X-PRUTS-SOURCE:thehmm
X-PRUTS-UUID:thehmm:10049-1792839600-1792861200@thehmm.nl
X-PRUTS-RADAR-ID:thehmm:10049-1792839600-1792861200@thehmm.nl
X-PRUTS-OCCURRENCE-ID:thehmm:10049-1792839600-1792861200@thehmm.nl:2026-10-
 24T11:00:00+02:00
DTSTAMP:20261006T023755Z
SUMMARY:Symposihmm 📡 Capture & Control
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
DTSTART:20261024T090000Z
DTEND:20261024T150000Z
DESCRIPTION:With our second Symposihmm of the year\, Capture & Control\, we
 ’ll be exploring the entanglements between processes of physiological an
 d geological capture. How can we make sense of this paranoid era in online
  platforms? And how are our minds and our planet being captured\, rendered
 \, and manipulated?\n\nIn the 2000s\, Stanford University taught ‘captol
 ogy’\, a course on computers as persuasive technologies. It’s perhaps
  no surprise that Mike Krieger\, who went on to create Instagram\, took th
 is class. Over the past twenty years\, social media platforms have continu
 ed to perfect the tools of persuasion. They have figured out with extreme
  precision how to grab and hold our attention\, while simultaneously becom
 ing the main source of information for many people. Following these shifts
  online\, artist and researcher Trevor Paglen has argued that these platfo
 rms are the perfect place for “influence operations”\, referring to th
 e psyops (psychological operations) of secret services. Whereas back in th
 e day no one on the internet knew you were a dog\, today you can’t tell 
 who’s a bot\, troll\, or CIA agent.  A notion that can feed into conspi
 ratorial thinking and schizoposting. For Paglen\, we are currently moving 
 from surveillance capitalism into an era of psyops capitalism\, where our 
 behaviour is not only being tracked for profit\, but also manipulated and 
 steered. Others are calling this era\, and the online visual culture in it
 s wake\, “pysop realism”\, pointing to conspiracy minded posts rooted 
 in distrust and paranoia.\nWhile we comment\, post\, and scroll our way th
 rough social media—externalising our thoughts for data capture—the ram
 pant digitisation and replication of the earth and biological life is quie
 tly taking place. Companies like Quixel\, with over 18\,000 3D assets that
  they’ve captured—plants\, rocks\, logs\, coral reefs\, forest floors
 —sell our earth back to us to be used in gaming and other industries. Go
 ogle Maps has led to Google Earth has led to the end-goal of digitally cap
 turing the entire planet in order to create digital twins\, train AI world
  models\, ‘preserve’ habitats and monuments on the brink of collapse\,
  and build predictive systems. Palantir thrives on this data hoarding\, us
 ing it to analyse and create connections within disconnected data sets for
  militarisation and surveillance purposes. In the US\, Flock cameras scan 
 around 20 billion license plates every month\, tracking the movement of pe
 ople within cities and between states. And surveillance is expanding even 
 further\, onto our own bodies\, with Meta selling over 7 million pairs of 
 their Ray-Ban smart glasses in 2025\, a huge jump from previous years. The
  processes of capturing\, connecting\, and analysing every element on eart
 h is no longer a paranoid delusion\, it’s unfolding in front of our eyes
 . \n📋 Program\n11.00 – 12.30 \n\nWelcome and introduction\nTalk b
 y Daniël de Zeeuw\nTalk by Sophie Publig & Mikkel Rørbo\n\n12.30 – 13.
 15 \n\nLunch break\n\n13.15 – 15.00 \n\nHalf-Earth Socialism: A Plan
 etary Crisis Game with Francis Tseng\nThe Internet is a Horrible Place and
  I’m Here to Make it Worse: A workshop with Noura Tafeche\n\n15.15 –
  17.00 \n\nConversation with Marina Otero Verzier\nOne pixel\, many worl
 ds\, lecture-performance by Emilia Tapprest & Søren Siebel\n\nThroughout 
 the day there will be an exhibition with works by Nick Vyssotsky\, Constan
 t Dullaart\, and Tewa Barnosa. \nDuring the lunch break San Serriffe will
  have a pop-up bookshop selling publications from the speakers and artists
  and other books connected to the symposium’s themes. \nPlease note that
  the activities between 13.15 and 15.00 won’t be broadcast on our livest
 ream. Upon arrival at A Lab you can sign up for one of the activities on a
  first-come-first-served basis. \nFor Noura Tafeche’s workshop\, you wil
 l need to bring a laptop with you. Please note that this workshop includes
  discussion and analysis of implicit violent imagery and representations o
 f rape culture\, child abuse\, and self-harm. \n📅 Date: Saturday 24 Oct
 ober\n🕗 Time: 11.00 – 17.00 CEST\n📍 Location: A Lab and live
 stream on The Inbetween\n🎟️ Tickets: Onsite €27\,50/Student €20/
 Livestream 7\,50 \n♿ Accessibility \n\nAt the venue\, a wheelchair-acces
 sible ramp is available\, as well as a wheelchair lift. The lift needs to 
 be arranged in advance\, so please email us at info@thehmm.nl\, at least 3
  days before the event\, to let us know if you will need it. This allows u
 s to make sure everything is ready and accessible upon arrival.\nA Lab has
  an accessible MIVA (wheelchair-accessible) toilet that meets the applicab
 le accessibility requirements.\nDuring the event we can provide live close
 d captioning for those with hearing impairments and disabilities. Please r
 each out to us via info@thehmm.nl—at least 3 days before the event if po
 ssible—if you are joining on-site and have this access need\, so that we
  can reserve a seat for you within view of the screen with captions. If yo
 u are joining online via our livestream\, live captioning will be availabl
 e as one of the streaming modes.\n\n🩵 This programme is kindly supporte
 d by the Creative Industries Fund NL\, AFK and hosted by A Lab.
URL:https://thehmm.nl/event/symposihmm-%f0%9f%93%a1-capture-control/
CATEGORIES:The Hmm
END:VEVENT
